# Frontend Architecture — Namly

## Core Stack

- Next.js 16+ App Router
- React 19
- TypeScript strict mode
- Tailwind CSS
- TanStack Query
- React Hook Form
- Zod
- shadcn/ui
- Motion
- Vaul
- Sonner

---

## Product Context

Namly is a mobile-first food continuity and meal planning platform.

The product goal is NOT calorie tracking obsession.

The goal is:
- continuity
- low friction
- emotional clarity
- habit support
- simple daily food organization

The UX must feel:
- soft
- calm
- lightweight
- highly scannable
- emotionally friendly

Avoid:
- dashboard feeling
- dense enterprise UI
- spreadsheet UX
- overly clinical flows

---

## Architecture Rules

Use feature-first architecture.

Structure:

src/
├── app/
├── features/
├── components/
├── components/ui/
├── lib/
├── providers/
├── hooks/
├── styles/
├── types/
└── constants/

Feature structure:

features/
└── feature-name/
    ├── components/
    ├── hooks/
    ├── services/
    ├── queries/
    ├── types/
    ├── utils/
    ├── constants/
    └── schemas/

---

## App Router Rules

- Use Server Components by default
- Use Client Components only when necessary
- Avoid unnecessary "use client"
- Keep routing logic inside app/
- Keep business logic inside features/

Do NOT use outdated Pages Router patterns.

---

## Data Fetching Rules

Use:
- TanStack Query
- centralized API client
- query hooks
- invalidation patterns

Avoid:
- direct fetch inside UI components
- duplicated loading states
- prop drilling refresh logic

---

## Global State Rules

Context API is ONLY allowed for:
- auth
- theme
- language
- lightweight app preferences

Do NOT use Context for server state.

Avoid Redux and Zustand unless absolutely necessary.

---

## Forms

Always use:
- React Hook Form
- Zod

Requirements:
- reusable field components
- accessible inputs
- mobile-friendly interactions
- progressive disclosure

Avoid giant forms.

---

## API Rules

Backend:
- NestJS REST API
- JWT Bearer auth
- Supabase Auth Google OAuth

Frontend rules:
- centralized API client
- centralized token handling
- centralized refresh handling
- no duplicated auth validation

---

## Component Rules

- Keep components small
- Separate UI from logic
- Prefer composition
- Avoid giant props interfaces
- Avoid god components

Split:
- visual components
- hooks
- services
- state logic

---

## Mobile-First Rules

This product is primarily mobile.

Prioritize:
- thumb-friendly interactions
- bottom sheets
- low tap count
- strong hierarchy
- quick scanning
- smooth transitions

Avoid:
- hover interactions
- dense tables
- desktop-first layouts
- modal-heavy UX

---

## Calendar System

IMPORTANT:

The calendar system must be reusable across:
- planner
- history
- future analytics

Do NOT create:
- planner-specific calendars
- history-specific calendars

Instead create reusable primitives:
- date navigation
- day indicators
- weekly rendering
- monthly rendering
- swipe navigation
- temporal state logic

Calendar architecture should support:
- weekly views
- monthly views
- timeline views
- activity indicators
- meal states

---

## Rendering Philosophy

Build:
- reusable primitives
- reusable cards
- reusable timeline items

Avoid:
- screen-specific duplicated components

Good:
calendar/
timeline/
meal/
feedback/

Bad:
home-components/
planner-components/
history-components/

---

## TypeScript Rules

- strict mode
- no any
- explicit domain types
- avoid unsafe casting
- use import type where possible

---

## Styling Rules

Use Tailwind only.

Avoid:
- CSS modules
- styled-components
- inline random styles

Use:
- semantic spacing
- reusable utility patterns
- composable variants

---

## UX Philosophy

Each screen must have ONE primary purpose.

Home:
- daily continuity
- next actions
- progress visibility

History:
- temporal review

Planner:
- future organization

Recipes:
- inspiration + organization

Settings:
- low-frequency configuration

Avoid mixing multiple mental models in the same screen.

---

## Performance Rules

- Prefer server rendering for initial data
- Lazy load heavy interactions
- Avoid unnecessary re-renders
- Keep client boundaries small
- Optimize lists and timelines

---

## AI Assistant Behavior

When generating code:
- prioritize maintainability
- challenge poor architecture decisions
- avoid overengineering
- avoid premature abstractions
- think long-term product scalability
- optimize for readability
- optimize for mobile UX consistency

Never generate massive files when smaller composable files are better.